dma-buf: add the _unlocked vmap/vunmap wrappers

drm_gem_dma_helper.c:233 calls dma_buf_vunmap_unlocked() when freeing a GEM
object that came in over PRIME, and drm-kmod's dma-buf declares only the bare
dma_buf_vmap()/dma_buf_vunmap(). The DMA GEM helpers therefore fail to compile
with "call to undeclared function 'dma_buf_vunmap_unlocked'" -- one of the
five symbols blocking a KMS driver for the Pi 5 (nextbsd-kernel#176).

Upstream Linux split these in 6.2: dma_buf_vmap() asserts dma_resv_held() and
the _unlocked form takes the reservation lock around it. This port never made
that split -- dma_buf_vmap() and dma_buf_vunmap() each take dmabuf->lock
themselves (dma-buf.c:726 and :759) and neither asserts the reservation lock.

So these are aliases, deliberately. They do NOT take dma_resv_lock() the way
dma_buf_map_attachment_unlocked() a few lines above does, because the
functions they wrap already do their own locking, and wrapping them in a
second unrelated lock would invent an ordering the locked forms know nothing
about. The name is the only thing being added.

--- a/linuxkpi/gplv2/include/linux/dma-buf.h
+++ b/linuxkpi/gplv2/include/linux/dma-buf.h
@@ -189,4 +189,25 @@
 int dma_buf_vmap(struct dma_buf *dmabuf, struct iosys_map *map);
 void dma_buf_vunmap(struct dma_buf *dmabuf, struct iosys_map *map);
 
+/*
+ * The _unlocked forms the DRM helpers call. Upstream these take the
+ * reservation lock around a dma_buf_vmap() that asserts dma_resv_held(); this
+ * port's dma_buf_vmap()/dma_buf_vunmap() instead take dmabuf->lock themselves
+ * and assert nothing, so there is no second lock to acquire and none is taken
+ * here on purpose.
+ */
+static inline int
+dma_buf_vmap_unlocked(struct dma_buf *dmabuf, struct iosys_map *map)
+{
+
+	return (dma_buf_vmap(dmabuf, map));
+}
+
+static inline void
+dma_buf_vunmap_unlocked(struct dma_buf *dmabuf, struct iosys_map *map)
+{
+
+	dma_buf_vunmap(dmabuf, map);
+}
+
 #endif /* _LINUX_GPLV2_DMA_BUF_H_ */
